    slug: "le-corbusier",
    biography:
      "Charles-Édouard Jeanneret was born in 1887 in La Chaux-de-Fonds, a Swiss watchmaking town whose precision craft culture left a lasting mark on him. He trained first as an engraver at his father's trade before a teacher, Charles L'Eplattenier, steered him toward architecture — a discipline he would go on to learn almost entirely through travel, observation and self-directed study rather than formal schooling.\n\nA formative journey through the Balkans, Greece and Italy in 1911 exposed him to the Parthenon and to the stripped, geometric clarity of Mediterranean vernacular building, impressions that would resurface throughout his career. Settling in Paris in 1917, he adopted the pseudonym Le Corbusier and aligned himself with Purism, co-founding the journal L'Esprit Nouveau and publishing Vers une architecture in 1923 — a manifesto arguing that houses should be designed with the same rigor as aircraft and ocean liners, built around his famous assertion that a house is \"a machine for living in.\"\n\nThroughout the 1920s he formulated his Five Points of Architecture — pilotis, free plan, free façade, ribbon windows and roof gardens — and realised them most completely in the Villa Savoye outside Paris. Working alongside his cousin Pierre Jeanneret and, from 1927, Charlotte Perriand, he also turned to furniture, producing the LC collection of tubular steel seating that remains in production with Cassina today. After the Second World War his architecture shifted toward the raw, sculptural concrete of Béton Brut, realised at the scale of an entire building in the Unité d'Habitation in Marseille and at the scale of a city in his master plan for Chandigarh, India.\n\nLe Corbusier died in 1965, swimming near his cabin on the French Riviera. Few architects have been as influential, or as contested: his urban planning ideas reshaped postwar housing policy worldwide, for better and for worse, while his furniture and his individual buildings remain foundational reference points for modernist design.",
