    biography:
      "Charlotte Perriand was born in Paris in 1903 and trained in furniture design at the École de l'Union Centrale des Arts Décoratifs, graduating directly into the city's modernist ferment. In 1927 she presented Bar sous le toit, a chrome-and-aluminium bar installation for her own apartment, at the Salon d'Automne — a work so confidently industrial that it persuaded Le Corbusier to reverse his earlier refusal to hire her, famously having told her \"we don't embroider cushions here.\"\n\nFor the following decade she worked inside Le Corbusier's studio alongside Pierre Jeanneret, where the three co-developed the LC collection of tubular steel furniture, including the reclining LC4 chaise longue — though Perriand's individual authorship of this work went largely uncredited for decades, attributed instead almost entirely to Le Corbusier. She left the studio in 1937, increasingly drawn to natural materials and a more human, less purely industrial design language.\n\nAn extended period working as a design advisor to the Japanese government from 1940 exposed her to traditional joinery, bamboo and a different relationship between furniture and the body, lastingly reorienting her practice away from cold metal toward wood and woven materials. Returning to France, she continued to design furniture, interiors and exhibitions for the rest of her career, culminating in a near two-decade commission to design the interiors of the Les Arcs ski resort in the French Alps, a project she led almost entirely on her own terms.\n\nPerriand died in Paris in 1999. Long overshadowed by her male collaborators despite having co-designed some of the century's most reproduced furniture, she has since been the subject of major retrospectives restoring her name to work it had quietly been removed from.",
