      "Eero Saarinen was born in 1910 in Kirkkonummi, Finland, the son of the prominent architect Eliel Saarinen. The family emigrated to the United States in 1923 when his father was invited to teach, eventually founding the Cranbrook Academy of Art in Michigan — an institution Eero grew up inside of and would later teach at himself, forming the tight creative circle that included Charles Eames, Ray Kaiser and Florence Schust.\n\nAfter studying sculpture in Paris and architecture at Yale, Saarinen worked initially with his father before striking out with his own increasingly distinct voice. His partnership with Charles Eames produced the prize-winning Organic Design in Home Furnishings entries for a 1940 MoMA competition, early experiments in moulded plywood that anticipated the Eameses' own later furniture. Saarinen's own furniture career flourished through Knoll, where Florence Knoll commissioned the Womb Chair in 1948 and, in 1956, the Pedestal collection — a radical rethinking of table and chair legs into a single sculptural stem, designed to eliminate what he called \"the slum of legs.\"\n\nAs an architect, Saarinen refused to repeat a signature style, instead tailoring each building's form to its specific purpose: the swooping, flight-inspired concrete shell of the TWA Flight Center at New York's Idlewild Airport, the soaring stainless-steel Gateway Arch in St. Louis, and the glass-and-steel rigor of the General Motors Technical Center. This range made him one of the most sought-after architects of 1950s America, even as it occasionally drew criticism for lacking a consistent personal style.\n\nSaarinen died suddenly of a brain tumour in 1961 at only fifty-one, midway through several major projects, including the TWA terminal and Washington Dulles International Airport, both completed posthumously by his office. His premature death cut short what was, by any measure, one of the most daring and varied careers in twentieth-century American design.",
