    biography:
      "Charles Eames was born in St. Louis, Missouri, in 1907 and studied architecture at Washington University before leaving, by most accounts pushed out for design views considered too modern for the curriculum of the time. He opened his own architecture practice in St. Louis through the early 1930s before an invitation to Cranbrook Academy of Art in Michigan brought him into the orbit of Eliel and Eero Saarinen, where he began experimenting seriously with moulded plywood furniture.\n\nAt Cranbrook he also met Ray Kaiser, a painter, whom he married in 1941; the couple moved to Los Angeles and founded the Eames Office, working from then on as equal, inseparable creative partners even as public credit would often default to Charles alone. Early plywood experiments, partly developed through wartime contracts producing moulded leg splints for the US Navy, matured into the Eames moulded plywood chair of 1946, prized by MoMA as the most important design of the twentieth century, and eventually the 1956 Eames Lounge Chair and Ottoman, designed with Herman Miller to feel, in Charles's words, like \"a well-worn baseball mitt.\"\n\nThe Eameses' work extended well beyond furniture: in 1949 they built their own home in Pacific Palisades as part of the Case Study House program, assembled almost entirely from off-the-shelf industrial parts ordered from catalogues, proving that prefabrication could be composed into something warm rather than merely efficient. Through the Eames Office they also produced dozens of short films, multimedia exhibitions and toys, treating communication and education as design problems no less serious than a chair.\n\nCharles Eames died in St. Louis in 1978. His furniture, developed and produced in genuine partnership with Ray across nearly four decades, remains among the most widely recognised and continuously manufactured design in the world.",
