    biography:
      "Ray Eames was born Ray Kaiser in Sacramento, California, in 1912 and trained as a painter, studying under the abstract expressionist Hans Hofmann in New York and exhibiting with the American Abstract Artists group — a serious early career in fine art that is often overshadowed by her later design work. In 1940 she enrolled at Cranbrook Academy of Art in Michigan, drawn there to study under the same circle of designers gathering around Eliel Saarinen, where she met Charles Eames.\n\nThe two married in 1941 and moved to Los Angeles, founding the Eames Office as full creative partners, a fact obscured for decades by a design press and public more comfortable crediting a male \"genius\" than a married couple working jointly. Ray's training as a painter shaped the Office's furniture as much as any engineering decision: her sense of colour, proportion and composition is visible in the textiles, graphics and finished silhouettes of the moulded plywood and fibreglass chairs that bear the Eames name, and she took the lead on the furniture's upholstery and colourways.\n\nBeyond furniture, Ray directed much of the Eames Office's prolific output of exhibitions, toys and films, exercising close control over pacing, composition and visual clarity. The short film Powers of Ten, released in 1977 and co-credited to both Charles and Ray, distils this sensibility into nine minutes that zoom from a Chicago picnic to the edge of the observable universe and back into the atomic structure of a hand, still shown in classrooms worldwide as a model of visual explanation.\n\nRay continued running the Eames Office alone after Charles's death in 1978, overseeing its archive and legacy for another decade. She died in 1988, on the tenth anniversary of his death almost to the day, closing one of American design's most complete and most unevenly credited creative partnerships.",
