      "Alvar Aalto was born in Kuortane, Finland, in 1898 and studied architecture at the Helsinki University of Technology, graduating in 1921 into a young nation still forging its own cultural identity after independence from Russia in 1917. He opened his first practice in Jyväskylä in 1923, and in 1924 married architect Aino Marsio, who became his closest professional collaborator and co-designer until her death in 1949.\n\nHis international reputation was established by the Paimio Sanatorium, completed in 1933, a tuberculosis hospital in which Aalto treated the entire building as a medical instrument: ward orientation was calculated for maximum sunlight, door closers were engineered to operate silently, and wash basins were shaped specifically to minimise splashing noise. The project's accompanying bent-plywood Paimio Chair, designed to ease patients' breathing through its reclined angle, became the first widely celebrated expression of what would become his signature material. In 1935, with Aino and two collaborators, he founded the furniture company Artek to manufacture and distribute this bent-wood furniture on his own terms rather than through a licensing arrangement.\n\nAalto's architecture consistently rejected the harder, more mechanical language of his Central European contemporaries in favour of organic forms, natural materials and close attention to how light moved through a building — qualities visible in buildings as different in scale as the Villa Mairea private residence and Finlandia Hall, a major concert and conference venue in Helsinki. His furniture followed the same humanist logic, most famously in the rippled hand-blown glass Savoy Vase, designed in 1936 for the opening of Helsinki's Savoy restaurant and still produced by Iittala today.\n\nAalto remained active until his death in Helsinki in 1976, having built extensively across Finland and internationally and having trained a generation of Finnish architects in his belief that good design should answer human needs before formal ones.",
