    slug: "isamu-noguchi",
    biography:
      "Isamu Noguchi was born in Los Angeles in 1904 to an American writer, Léonie Gilmour, and a Japanese poet, Yone Noguchi, and spent much of his childhood in Japan before returning to the United States for schooling, an early cross-cultural displacement that shaped his lifelong preoccupation with how a single body of work could belong to more than one tradition at once. He initially trained toward a career in medicine before abandoning it for sculpture, studying under Gutzon Borglum before a 1927 Guggenheim Fellowship brought him to Paris, where he briefly apprenticed in the studio of Constantin Brâncuși, absorbing the older sculptor's commitment to pared-down, essential form.\n\nReturning to New York, Noguchi supported himself partly through portrait busts of prominent figures while developing his own sculptural language, and by the 1930s and 1940s was working across an unusually wide range of disciplines: public plazas, stage sets for the choreographer Martha Graham, and, from 1944, furniture. His glass-topped biomorphic Noguchi Table, designed for Herman Miller, treated a piece of furniture as a freestanding sculptural object in its own right rather than a purely functional form.\n\nFrom 1951 he began a long-running collaboration with craftsmen in Gifu, Japan, producing the Akari series of paper and bamboo light sculptures — lanterns descended from traditional Japanese chōchin lamps that Noguchi reconceived as sculptural objects for modern interiors, eventually numbering well over a hundred variations produced across his lifetime. In parallel he continued large-scale public work, including sculpture gardens, plazas and playgrounds across the United States, Israel and Japan, treating landscape design as inseparable from sculpture.\n\nIn 1985, Noguchi converted a former industrial building across from his Long Island City studio into a museum dedicated entirely to his own work, arranging its galleries and sculpture garden himself. He died in New York in 1988, having worked fluidly across sculpture, furniture, stage design and public space for six decades without treating any of them as a lesser discipline than the others.",
