    biography:
      "Jean Prouvé was born in 1901 in Nancy, France, into a family closely associated with the École de Nancy, an Art Nouveau movement his father had helped found, though Prouvé's own instincts ran toward engineering and industrial process rather than decorative craft. He trained as a metalworker and blacksmith rather than through formal architectural or design education, opening his own metalworking workshop in Nancy in 1923 and initially producing ornamental ironwork before shifting decisively toward furniture and architectural components.\n\nProuvé's furniture was governed by a consistent structural logic: every part was sized and shaped according to the load it actually had to carry, visible most clearly in his 1934 Standard Chair, whose rear legs are deliberately more reinforced than its front legs because that is where a sitter's weight falls. This approach, closer to engineering than conventional furniture design, produced pieces that were inexpensive to manufacture at scale and that have since become some of the most sought-after French furniture of the twentieth century, including the Compas desk and the Cité armchair.\n\nHis ambitions extended well beyond furniture into full building systems: through the 1930s and 1940s he developed folded and pressed sheet steel and aluminium construction techniques intended for rapid, affordable prefabrication, culminating in the 1949–1951 Maison Tropicale, a prefabricated aluminium house designed to be manufactured in France and assembled on site in colonial West Africa, built from the same structural honesty as his furniture. Only three were ever built, though decades later they became some of the most expensive houses ever sold at auction.\n\nProuvé also served briefly as mayor of Nancy following the Liberation of France in 1944, reflecting the respect he commanded locally well beyond his design work, and later chaired the jury that selected Renzo Piano and Richard Rogers's design for the Centre Pompidou in 1971. He died in Nancy in 1984; his work, long undervalued relative to his contemporaries, has since become some of the most sought-after design of the twentieth century.",
