    biography:
      "Marcel Breuer was born in 1902 in Pécs, Hungary, and enrolled at the Bauhaus in Weimar in 1920 at just eighteen years old, initially in the carpentry workshop under Walter Gropius's newly founded school. His talent was recognised quickly: by 1925, at only twenty-four, he was appointed head of the Bauhaus furniture workshop in Dessau, where he designed the Wassily Chair, a tubular steel and canvas seat reportedly inspired by the handlebars of his bicycle, among the first pieces of furniture to use bent steel tubing as a structural material rather than a decorative one.\n\nHis 1928 Cesca Chair, a cantilevered, cane-seated design produced without back legs, pushed the same material logic further and became one of the most widely copied chairs of the century, still in production and still frequently imitated today. Breuer left the Bauhaus in 1928 to establish his own architecture and design practice in Berlin, continuing to develop tubular steel furniture while taking on increasingly significant architectural commissions across Europe through the early 1930s.\n\nLike many Bauhaus figures, Breuer fled Nazi Germany, settling first in London and then, in 1937, moving to the United States at Walter Gropius's invitation to teach at Harvard's Graduate School of Design, where the two briefly partnered in practice and where Breuer shaped a generation of architecture students including Philip Johnson, I. M. Pei and Paul Rudolph. His American architectural career shifted decisively away from his early lightweight furniture toward the heavy, sculptural concrete language that became known as Brutalism.\n\nThis later direction culminated in his 1966 Whitney Museum of American Art building in New York, an inverted granite-clad concrete volume that widens as it rises, deliberately fortress-like against the glass towers of Madison Avenue — a building as polarising on completion as it remains today. Breuer died in New York in 1981, having moved across his career from the lightest tubular steel furniture to some of the heaviest concrete architecture of twentieth-century modernism.",
