    slug: "michael-thonet",
    biography:
      "Michael Thonet was born in 1796 in Boppard, a small town on the Rhine, and trained as a cabinetmaker, opening his own workshop there in the 1820s. Through the 1830s he began experimenting with techniques for steam-bending solid wood into curved forms without the carving or joinery that conventional furniture-making required, a technical breakthrough that would eventually let a chair be assembled from a small number of simple bent components rather than many carved and joined parts.\n\nHis early bentwood furniture attracted the attention of the Austrian State Chancellor, Prince Metternich, who invited Thonet to Vienna in 1842, where he secured an imperial patent protecting his bending process. In 1853 he founded Gebrüder Thonet with his five sons, establishing the specialised factories that would mass-produce his furniture designs at a scale no previous furniture maker had attempted, with components manufactured in standardised, interchangeable form and shipped unassembled to be put together locally — an industrial logic that would not look out of place in a flat-pack furniture catalogue more than a century later.\n\nThis system reached its definitive expression in 1859 with Chair No. 14, assembled from just six wooden components, two screws and ten nuts, designed to be shipped efficiently in large quantities and sold at a fraction of the cost of carved, joined furniture. Chair No. 14 became the ubiquitous seating of European cafés and became known as the \"consumer chair\"; by the start of the twentieth century, Gebrüder Thonet had produced tens of millions of them, exported across Europe and beyond, making it very plausibly the best-selling chair design in history.\n\nThonet died in Vienna in 1871, having built an industrial furniture-making template his sons would continue to expand for decades. That combination of industrial efficiency and elegant, minimal form makes him a direct ancestor of the furniture designers of the following century, several of whom, including Le Corbusier, Mies van der Rohe and Marcel Breuer, would later have their own tubular steel furniture manufactured by the very same company he founded.",
