    slug: "dieter-rams",
    biography:
      "Dieter Rams was born in 1932 in Wiesbaden, Germany, and grew up partly in the household of his carpenter grandfather during the Second World War, an early exposure to craft and material that he later credited with shaping his eye for honest construction. He studied architecture and interior design at the Wiesbaden School of Art, graduating in 1953 and briefly working for an architecture firm before joining the electronics manufacturer Braun in 1955, initially as an architect designing the company's buildings and exhibition spaces.\n\nWithin a few years Rams moved into product design proper, and by 1961 had become Braun's chief design officer, a position he held for roughly three decades and from which he shaped the visual identity of an entire generation of consumer electronics. His 1956 SK4 record player and radio combination, nicknamed \"Snow White's Coffin\" for its clear acrylic lid, was an early, defining statement of the restrained, functional design language that would become Braun's signature and, in time, one of the most influential visual vocabularies in twentieth-century product design.\n\nWorking closely with a small, consistent team, Rams and his colleagues produced calculators, shavers, coffee makers and audio equipment built around the same underlying discipline: visual clarity, minimal ornamentation, and components arranged according to function rather than appearance. In parallel, from 1960, he designed the modular 606 Universal Shelving System for the furniture company Vitsöe, a wall-mounted kit of parts intended to be extended or reconfigured indefinitely rather than replaced, and still produced to the same specification today.\n\nFrustrated, in his words, by \"a world of unnecessary products,\" Rams formulated his now widely cited Ten Principles of Good Design during the 1970s and 1980s, articulating a philosophy of restraint that would later become explicitly influential on Apple's product design under Jony Ive. He retired from Braun in 1995 but has remained an active, frequently consulted voice in design discourse well into his nineties, his insistence on \"as little design as possible\" more widely cited today than at any point during his working career.",
