    biography:
      "Gio Ponti was born in Milan in 1891 and studied architecture at the Politecnico di Milano, his education interrupted by service in the First World War before he graduated in 1921. He began his career designing ceramics for the Richard-Ginori manufacturer, work recognised internationally at the 1925 Paris Exposition, before expanding into architecture, furniture, lighting, glassware and cutlery — a breadth of practice he maintained for the rest of his life, refusing to specialise in any single discipline or scale.\n\nIn 1928 he founded and began editing Domus, a magazine covering architecture and design that remains, nearly a century later, one of the field's most influential publications, giving Ponti an unusually direct platform to shape Italian design taste well beyond his own individual projects. Through Domus and his own practice he became a central figure in establishing Milan as a capital of postwar design, championing a generation of Italian architects and designers alongside his own prolific output.\n\nHis furniture work reached its best-known expression in the 1957 Superleggera chair for Cassina, refined over years from a traditional Chiavari fishing chair into an almost impossibly light ash-wood seat weighing under two kilograms, famously demonstrated in advertising by having a child lift it with one finger. In architecture, his 1960 Pirelli Tower in Milan, designed with structural engineer Pier Luigi Nervi, was among the tallest reinforced concrete buildings in the world on completion, its tapered hexagonal profile breaking sharply from the rectangular glass towers dominating postwar skylines elsewhere.\n\nPonti continued designing prolifically across every scale into his late eighties, including the Villa Planchart in Caracas and extensive work in ceramics and glass, guided throughout by a consistent belief in everyday elegance over monumental statement. He died in Milan in 1979, having shaped Italian design taste for most of the twentieth century, as both a practising designer and as an editor and tastemaker.",
